      "page_title": "Auth Code + PKCE: The Flow to Use",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. PKCE Mechanics",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "v = base64url(random(32))",
            "// 43-128 chars, CSPRNG only",
            "ch = base64url(sha256(v))",
            "code_challenge_method=S256"
          ],
          "note": "never plain, never Math.random"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Why PKCE works",
          "text": "A stolen `code` — grabbed off a mobile custom URL scheme or out of the browser — is **useless** without the verifier. OAuth 2.1 makes PKCE mandatory for ==confidential clients too==, not just public ones."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Worked Example",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "http",
          "lines": [
            "GET /authorize?response_type=code",
            "&client_id=app&scope=openid",
            "&redirect_uri=https://a/cb",
            "&state=Xk9&code_challenge=E9M..",
            "&code_challenge_method=S256"
          ],
          "note": "front channel: no secret here"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "http",
          "lines": [
            "302 https://a/cb?code=SplxlO",
            "&state=Xk9   # compare!",
            "",
            "POST /token",
            "grant_type=authorization_code",
            "&code=SplxlO&code_verifier=dBj.."
          ],
          "note": "code dies in 30-60s, single use"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Skipping `state` compare on return — open **CSRF** / login fixation",
            "`Math.random()` for `state` or verifier — not a __CSPRNG__",
            "Verifier in `localStorage` — another tab or origin reads it",
            "Reusing a `code`: AS must **revoke the whole grant**",
            "Treating PKCE as a `state` replacement — different attacks"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "state vs PKCE",
          "items": [
            "PKCE stops **code theft** at redemption",
            "`state` stops **CSRF** / login fixation",
            "Both mandatory, both single-use",
            "Store in `sessionStorage` or HttpOnly cookie"
          ],
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Summary",
          "text": "Verifier stays home, challenge goes out, `code` comes back, `/token` proves possession. Check `state` byte-for-byte before you spend the code."
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "TV asks",
                "sub": "device_code"
              },
              {
                "text": "Shows code",
                "sub": "BXQK-2FTZ"
              },
              {
                "text": "Phone visits",
                "sub": "verification_uri"
              },
              {
                "text": "TV polls",
                "sub": "gets tokens",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "subtitle": "OAuth 2.1 default"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Client Credentials, Device Code & The Dead Grants",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What Else Is There?",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Code+PKCE covers users. What runs when __no user is present__?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "==client_credentials==: app is its own resource owner",
            "Auth with client_id/secret, ==mTLS== or `private_key_jwt`",
            "**Never** issues a refresh token — just re-request",
            "Scopes come from client config, not consent"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "POST /token",
            "grant_type=client_credentials",
            "&scope=invoices.read",
            "# auth: Basic base64(id:secret)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Device Code (RFC 8628)",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "How does a **smart TV** with no keyboard log you in?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Device POSTs `/device_authorization`, gets `user_code`",
            "TV shows code + ==verification_uri==; you use your phone",
            "Device polls `/token` on `device_code` until approved",
            "Honour `authorization_pending` and back off on `slow_down`"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "{\"error\":\"authorization_pending\"}",
            "# keep polling at interval",
            "{\"error\":\"slow_down\"}",
            "# interval += 5 seconds"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Refresh Is a Grant Too",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "http",
          "lines": [
            "POST /token",
            "grant_type=refresh_token",
            "&refresh_token=v1.Mr9...",
            "&client_id=spa-web"
          ],
          "note": "back channel only, see page 9"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. The Dead Ones",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Why did **implicit** and **ROPC** get killed?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "{red:implicit} `response_type=token`: token lands in URL __fragment__",
            "Fragment leaks via history, Referer, logs — and no refresh",
            "{red:ROPC}: client handles the user's ==password== directly",
            "ROPC breaks MFA, SSO and federation — no redirect, no prompt"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Reaching for ROPC to 'simplify' first-party login",
            "Six months later: security wants **MFA** — ROPC has no UI to show it",
            "Then sales wants **SSO** — no redirect, so no federation",
            "Migration means rewriting the login screen anyway"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "key",
      "subtitle": "Machines, TVs and the dead"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Token Exchange & Picking the Right Grant",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Token Exchange (8693)",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Service A holds a user token and must call B. Now what?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Trade the incoming token for a ==downscoped== one",
            "Keeps `sub` (delegation), drops scopes and swaps `aud`",
            "Better than forwarding the user's token blindly"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "grant_type=urn:ietf:params:",
            "  oauth:grant-type:token-exchange",
            "&subject_token=eyJ...",
            "&audience=billing-api"
          ]
        },
        {
